@@ -121,10 +122,11 @@ Root of the repo also holds a sequence of `git format-patch` files (`R1..R6-*.pa
   - `IncludeXmlComments` is now wired for all three XML-doc-producing assemblies (Presentation, ApplicationLayer, DomainLayer — the latter two had `GenerateDocumentationFile` enabled for the first time in this pass). Every layer's existing `<summary>`/`<param>`/`<response>` doc comments now reach Swagger UI; previously none of them did, regardless of how complete they were in source.
   - `EnumSchemaDescriptionsFilter` (`ISchemaFilter`) appends each enum's numeric wire value + member doc to its schema description, reading the same XML doc files via `XmlDocIndex` — needed because no `JsonStringEnumConverter` is registered anywhere (enums serialize as plain integers; see §12 enum persistence decision) and Swashbuckle's built-in XML support documents the enum type, not each member.
   - `ExamplesOperationFilter` attaches named, multi-scenario request/response examples per action, looked up from `ExampleCatalog.All` by `(Controller, Action)`. **To extend for a new controller:** add one `Swagger/Examples/{Module}Examples.cs` file (mirrors `Errors/{Module}Errors.cs` — one file per module) building an `EndpointExampleSet` per action via `EndpointExampleSetBuilder`, then add one `Merge(catalog, {Module}Examples.Build());` line in `ExampleCatalog.Build()`. Examples are plain C# objects (anonymous types), not hand-built `IOpenApiAny` graphs — `OpenApiAnyFactory` does that conversion.
-  - **Coverage as of this pass:** foundation (XML comments, enum filter, examples filter/catalog) applies to every controller already. Full example content exists for `AuthController`, `TenantsController` (Phase S1), and `BranchController`, `ProductController`, `ProductPrintConfigController`, `PrintersController`, `PrintImagesController` (Phase S2). Remaining controllers are planned in phases S3 (Stock/ProductItems/Inventory upload/InventoryHistory/CardFiles), S4 (Transactions/CardDisposals), S5 (BranchRequests) — same one-module-per-file convention.
-  - **A genuine finding from this pass, not a bug fix:** `[Authorize]`/`[Authorize(Policy=...)]` failures (missing/invalid token, wrong policy) are handled by ASP.NET Core's authorization middleware *before* an action runs, and return an **empty body** — not the `ApiResponse<T>` envelope. Several controllers' `[ProducesResponseType]` on 401/403 previously claimed a typed envelope body for these regardless. `AuthController`/`TenantsController` were corrected in Phase S1, and `BranchController`/`ProductController`/`PrintersController`/`ProductPrintConfigController`/`PrintImagesController` in Phase S2 (XML docs, not `[ProducesResponseType]` types, since S2's admin-gated write actions genuinely *do* return the enveloped body — see next bullet). **The same audit is still needed on the remaining controllers** as S3-S5 land — check each 401/403 against its actual service code before trusting the existing `[ProducesResponseType]` type.
+  - **Coverage as of this pass:** foundation (XML comments, enum filter, examples filter/catalog) applies to every controller already. Full example content exists for `AuthController`, `TenantsController` (S1); `BranchController`, `ProductController`, `ProductPrintConfigController`, `PrintersController`, `PrintImagesController` (S2); and `StockController`, `ProductItemsController`, `InventoryController` (batch upload), `InventoryHistoryController`, `CardFilesController` (S3). Remaining controllers are planned in S4 (Transactions/CardDisposals) and S5 (BranchRequests) — same one-module-per-file convention.
+  - **A genuine finding from this pass, not a bug fix:** `[Authorize]`/`[Authorize(Policy=...)]` failures (missing/invalid token, wrong policy) are handled by ASP.NET Core's authorization middleware *before* an action runs, and return an **empty body** — not the `ApiResponse<T>` envelope. Several controllers' `[ProducesResponseType]` on 401/403 previously claimed a typed envelope body for these regardless. Corrected on `AuthController`/`TenantsController` (S1), `BranchController`/`ProductController`/`PrintersController`/`ProductPrintConfigController`/`PrintImagesController` (S2), and `StockController`/`ProductItemsController`/`InventoryController`/`InventoryHistoryController`/`CardFilesController` (S3) — the last two continue the "rare enveloped-401 edge case" pattern (`Batch.ActorNotResolved` on upload; `CardFile.ActorNotResolved` on generation), same shape as `Tenant.ActorNotResolved`. **The same audit is still needed on the remaining controllers** as S4-S5 land — check each 401/403 against its actual service code before trusting the existing `[ProducesResponseType]` type.
   - **A second finding, S2:** the Printing module's admin-only write actions (`PrintersController`'s Create/Update/Delete/Restore, `ProductPrintConfigController`'s Update/GetFull, `PrintImagesController`'s Upload/Replace/MigrateLegacyStorage) enforce "system-admin only" in the *service* (`PrintingErrors.*OnlySystemAdmin()`, `ErrorCategory.Forbidden`), not via an authorization policy — so unlike `TenantsController`'s 403, these ones genuinely *are* the standard enveloped `ApiResponse<T>` body. `BranchController`/`ProductController` similarly have a rare enveloped-401 edge case (`Branch.ActorNotResolved` / reused `Auth.ActorNotResolved`) reachable from `Create`/`Delete`/`Restore` when a token passes authentication but the acting identity can't be resolved from its claims — same shape and same rarity as `Tenant.ActorNotResolved` from S1.
   - **A third finding, S2:** `PrintImagesController.Upload`'s `409 Conflict` is not a failure envelope at all — on a duplicate file name it returns a normal *success* envelope (`ApiResponse<PrintImageResponse>.Ok(...)`) carrying the **existing** image's metadata, with the status code forced to 409. This is the only endpoint found so far where a non-2xx status doesn't carry an `ApiError`; documented explicitly in its Swagger example and XML doc so a client doesn't try to parse an `error` field that isn't there.
+  - **A finding from S3:** `CardFilesController.Generate`'s success response (200) is a raw binary file (`application/octet-stream`), not JSON — the hand-off metadata a JSON response would normally carry (`fileMac`, `cardCount`, `expectedRowCount`) travels in `X-File-Mac`/`X-Card-Count`/`X-Expected-Row-Count` response headers instead. No 200 Swagger example is registered for this action for that reason; the headers are documented in its `<response code="200">` doc instead. `StockController.GetByBranch` was also confirmed (by reading `StockService`) to never 404 on an unknown/out-of-scope `branchId` — it returns an empty page, since a branch-scoped list is inherently "whatever matches," not "the one thing at this id."
 
